flowchart BT

  subgraph INTRO["READ ME FIRST"]
    direction TB
    P0["`**What the paper does**
    MultiMat trains one AI on four views of each crystal:
    1. its atomic structure
    2. its electron energy spectrum (DOS)
    3. its 3D electron cloud (charge density)
    4. a computer-written text description
    Idea: if the AI learns that all four views describe
    the same material, it should understand materials better.`"]
    HOW["`**How to read this chart**
    Boxes at the bottom = evidence found in the paper.
    Rounded boxes above = what the authors claim.
    Arrow label = how well the evidence backs the claim.
    Dotted arrow = the evidence goes AGAINST the claim.
    Grey six-sided box = an unstated assumption
    the claim quietly depends on.`"]
    GL["`**Key terms**
    DOS: how many electron energy levels exist at each energy
    Charge density: where the electrons sit in 3D space
    Error (MAE): average gap between prediction and truth, lower is better
    CGCNN, PotNet: earlier AI models for crystals
    Pre-training: learning general patterns on lots of data first
    UMAP: squeezes many numbers into a 2D picture`"]
    subgraph LEG["Colour key for claims"]
      direction LR
      K1("Supported")
      K2("Partly supported")
      K3("Weakly supported")
      K4("Not supported or contradicted")
      K5["Evidence"]
      K6{{"Hidden assumption"}}
    end
  end

  subgraph T1["QUESTION 1: Does it predict material properties better?"]
    E1["`**E1. Table 1**
    Older AI (CGCNN) vs newer AI (PotNet):
    PotNet has 3 to 13% lower error`"]
    E2["`**E2. Table 1**
    Training with one extra view
    (spectrum or electron cloud)
    lowers error by 3 to 12% vs PotNet`"]
    E3["`**E3. Table 1**
    A cheap, computer-written text description
    helps just as much as the costly spectrum`"]
    E4["`**E4. Table 1**
    Using 3 or 4 views instead of 2:
    extra gain is about the size
    of run-to-run noise`"]
    E5["`**E5. Methods**
    Each test repeated 3 times on one data split.
    Not stated: were the test materials
    already seen during pre-training?`"]
    E13["`**E13. Reference 30**
    An earlier paper reports
    PotNet as the best model`"]

    C6("`**C6. Up to ~10% lower error than PotNet**
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    C7("`**C7. Gain as big as 5 years of progress**
    (from CGCNN to PotNet)
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    C8("`**C8. More views in training = better**
    Verdict: weakly supported`")
    C9("`**C9. Would improve any AI model**
    Only one model was tried
    Verdict: not supported`")
    C10("`**C10. Works well with small datasets**
    Never tested with different data sizes
    Verdict: weakly supported`")
    C18("`**C18. PotNet was the best model**
    Taken from an earlier paper
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    C1("`**C1. MAIN CLAIM: Best accuracy to date**
    MultiMat predicts material properties
    better than existing methods
    Verdict: partly supported`")

    A1{{"`Beating one model on the authors'
    own test = best in the whole field`"}}
    A2{{"`The gain comes from the extra information,
    not simply from extra training on crystals`"}}
    A3{{"`Test materials were not already
    seen during pre-training`"}}
    GAP["`**BIGGEST GAP IN THE PAPER**
    No test of pre-training on crystal structures alone.
    So we cannot tell if the gain comes from using
    several views, or just from extra training.`"]
  end

  subgraph T2["QUESTION 2: Can it find materials with a wanted behaviour?"]
    E8["`**E8. Fig. 3A**
    Given a spectrum, the AI picks out its own
    crystal among ~15,000 candidates
    (exact success rate not given in text)`"]
    E9["`**E9. Fig. 3B**
    Spectrum mismatch shrinks as more suggestions
    are allowed, but the best one is chosen by
    checking each suggestion's TRUE spectrum`"]
    E10["`**E10. Fig. 3C**
    Two hand-picked examples
    that match well`"]

    C13("`**C13. The four views line up well**
    inside the AI
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    C14("`**C14. Checking ~5 suggestions is enough**
    Verdict: weakly supported`")
    C15("`**C15. Will also work on bigger databases**
    A prediction, never tested
    Verdict: not supported`")
    C2("`**C2. MAIN CLAIM: Finds materials with a target spectrum**
    by searching for similar points in the AI's internal map
    Verdict: weakly supported`")
    C3("`**C3. MAIN CLAIM: Finds new, stable materials**
    Only already-known materials were searched,
    and stability was never checked
    Verdict: not supported`")

    A4{{"`The true spectrum of each suggestion is known,
    but in real discovery it would not be`"}}
    A5{{"`Every material in the
    database is stable`"}}
  end

  subgraph T3["QUESTION 3: Did the AI learn real physics?"]
    E11["`**E11. Fig. 4**
    2D map of the AI's internal features,
    coloured by crystal type, formation energy,
    and metal vs non-metal`"]
    C16("`**C16. Similar materials sit together on the map**
    A visual impression, no number given
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    C17("`**C17. Learned real physics, not noise**
    Verdict: weakly supported`")
    C4("`**C4. MAIN CLAIM: Features have physical meaning**
    and may give new scientific insight
    Verdict: weakly supported`")
    A6{{"`Clusters show learned physics, not just
    which chemical elements are present`"}}
  end

  subgraph T4["QUESTION 4: Does the training method itself work?"]
    E12["`**E12. Equations 3 and 4**
    Training formulas written out
    for 3 and 4 views`"]
    E7["`**E7. Data counts**
    Of ~155,000 crystals, only ~78,000
    have all views available`"]
    E6["`**E6. Table S1**
    Alternative all-at-once methods
    vs the pair-by-pair methods`"]
    C5("`**C5. MAIN CLAIM: Method extended from 2 to many views**
    Verdict: supported`")
    C11("`**C11. Pair-by-pair training can use incomplete data**
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    C12("`**C12. All-at-once methods are equally good**
    Their band-gap error is 6 to 9% worse
    Verdict: contradicted`")
  end

